.\" gtkdialog.1 — man page for gtk3sermo 1.0.0
.\" haplo-dialog <devel@haplo-dialog.fr>
.\" This file is installed as /usr/share/man/man1/gtkdialog.1
.\"
.TH GTKDIALOG 1 "June 2026" "gtk3sermo 1.0.0" "haplo-dialog User Commands"
.SH NAME
gtkdialog \- compatibility alias for gtk3sermo (GTK3 dialog box creator for shell scripts)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gtkdialog
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.br
.B gtkdialog
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.br
.B gtkdialog
\fB\-\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B gtkdialog
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
.br
.B VARIABLE=\(aq<window>...</window>\(aq gtkdialog
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gtkdialog
reads a GTK3 GUI description written in an XML-like language and displays
the resulting dialog window. It is designed to be called from shell scripts
to present interactive forms, file choosers, progress bars, list views,
and other widgets without the script needing any knowledge of GTK
programming.
.PP
The GUI description can be supplied from an environment variable
.RB ( \-\-program ),
a file
.RB ( \-\-file ),
or standard input
.RB ( \-\-stdin ).
.PP
When a button action produces output (EXIT, REFRESH, etc.), gtkdialog
prints the current value of each named widget to standard output in the
form
\fINAME\fR=\(dq\fIvalue\fR\(dq.
.PP
Those lines escape the four characters the shell expands inside double quotes
.RB ( \e ,
.BR \(dq ,
.B $
and the backtick), so the calling shell may
.B eval
them. Bear in mind that a field's value is typed by whoever
.I uses
the dialog, who is not necessarily whoever wrote the script: if that matters,
prefer
.B \-\-do
instead, which passes the values through the environment and never reads them
back as code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Enable debug mode. Each character read from the dialog description is echoed
to standard output as it is fed to the lexer. Useful for diagnosing parser
issues.
.TP
.BI \-p " VARIABLE" "\fR,\fP \-\-program=" VARIABLE
Read the GUI description from the shell environment variable
.IR VARIABLE .
This is the most common invocation style in scripts:
.RS
.PP
.nf
MYGUI='<window>...</window>'
gtkdialog --program=MYGUI
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-file=" FILE
Read the GUI description from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Read the GUI description from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-i " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-include=" FILE
Include and execute
.I FILE
before running the main GUI description.
.TP
.BI \-e " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-event-driven=" FILE
Execute
.I FILE
as an event-driven program. In this mode gtkdialog acts as a dispatcher,
re-invoking
.I FILE
for each widget event.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no-warning
Suppress non-fatal warning messages.
.TP
.BI \-G " GEOMETRY" "\fR,\fP \-\-geometry=" GEOMETRY
Set the initial window placement and size.
.I GEOMETRY
has the form
.RI [ W x H ][ + X + Y ]
following the X11 convention (e.g. \fB800x600+0+0\fR).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-center
Centre all windows on the screen.
.TP
.B \-\-print-ir
Parse the GUI description, print the internal representation, and exit
without displaying any window. Useful for debugging XML syntax errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-space-expand= STATE
Set the default
.I expand
packing state for all widgets.
.I STATE
is
.B true
or
.BR false .
.TP
.BI \-\-space-fill= STATE
Set the default
.I fill
packing state for all widgets.
.I STATE
is
.B true
or
.BR false .
.TP
.BI \-g " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-glade-xml=" FILE
Load a GtkBuilder UI file (.glade / .ui) instead of using the gtkdialog
XML language.
.SH EXIT STATUS
gtkdialog exits with status
.B 0
on normal termination. The exit value printed on standard output by an
EXIT action is a string, not a numeric exit code; the calling shell must
interpret it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all
Enable
.BR g_debug ()
messages (verbose runtime tracing). Combined with
.B \-\-debug
this gives maximum diagnostic output.
.TP
.B GTK_DEBUG=interactive
Open the GTK Inspector inside the running window
.RB ( Ctrl\-Shift\-I ).
.TP
.B GTK_THEME
Override the GTK theme, e.g.
.BR GTK_THEME=Adwaita:dark .
.TP
.B HAPLO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
When set (to any value), refuse the
.B /bin/sh \-c
fallback used for
.RB < input >/< action >
commands that contain shell metacharacters, and fail closed instead. Commands
without shell metacharacters still run via direct
.BR exec ()
(no shell). This is the same hardening switch documented in
.BR gtk3sermo (1).
.TP
.B GTKDIALOG_PIXMAP_PATH
Colon\-separated list of directories searched for image files that are not
found at the path given in the XML (used when loading
.BR <pixmap> ,
window and button icons, and menu\-item images).
.TP
.B MAIN_DIALOG
Fallback source variable. When none of
.BR \-\-program ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-\-stdin ,
.B \-\-event\-driven
or
.B \-\-glade\-xml
is given, the GUI description is read from the environment variable named
.BR MAIN_DIALOG .
.SH EXAMPLES
Display a minimal dialog from a shell variable:
.PP
.nf
DIALOG='
<window title="Hello">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Hello, world!</label></text>
    <button><label>OK</label><action>EXIT:ok</action></button>
  </vbox>
</window>'
gtkdialog --program=DIALOG
.fi
.PP
Read a dialog description from a file and capture the output:
.PP
.nf
eval $(gtkdialog --file=my_form.xml)
echo "User entered: $ENTRY"
.fi
.PP
Display a dialog from standard input (pipe):
.PP
.nf
cat my_form.xml | gtkdialog --stdin
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gtkdialog
The gtkdialog executable.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/gtkdialog/examples/
Shell script examples for each supported widget type.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B gtkdialog
is an upward-compatibility alias provided by the
.BR gtk3sermo (1)
package. On installation,
.B make install
creates the
.B gtkdialog
symlink to the
.B gtk3sermo
binary and this
.I gtkdialog.1
manual page as an alias of
.IR gtk3sermo.1 .
Legacy dialogs written for the original gtkdialog (XML descriptions,
command-line options, and the
.B \-\-program
environment-variable invocation) run unchanged and print their output in the
historical
.IR NAME =\(dq value \(dq
form. The underlying executable is named
.BR gtk3sermo ;
only gtk3sermo ships this alias, so there is no conflict between packages.
